  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to adapt the current optimization approach to lower resolution with modified balance between constraints. Our rationale is that satisfying the constraints exactly might be more critical than initially thought. The current implementation allows some tolerance for constraint deviations during optimization, but we need a strict enforcement mechanism. This approach aims to break local optima by heavily prioritizing constraint satisfaction, potentially leading to globally better solutions. The method involves training neural networks with fixed and potential fractional widths, then optimizing parameters for balanced constraint satisfaction. We expect that by strictly enforcing constraints, we can escape poorly converging solutions and find better optima.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nTensorFlow representation: \n  - Input tensor shape [num_samples, 3] (each sample has [width, height, channels])\n  - Output tensor shape [num_samples] (object class probabilities)\n\nObjective: Minimize classification loss (cross-entropy) while respecting dataset labels.\n\nConstraints and formulations:\nVolume preservation: total volume = input_volume (assumed fixed)\nInput shape transformation: Reshape only when necessary for batch processing or compatibility.\n\nImplementation steps derived from guiding principles will be detailed in the plan below.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   Project enhancement into the optimization framework:\n   - Add constraint preservation validation: Enforce both output and transformation confirmation for necessary conditions.\n   - Implement efficient K-fold cross-validation with data augmentation for better generalization.\n   - Optimize model hyperparameters with Bayesian optimization using Tree of Parzen Estimators.\n\nThis detailed procedural outline is structured below, followed by computation budget considerations.\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n   - Perform K-Fold cross-validation with K=5 for better model assessment.\n   - Implement data augmentation with various transformations (rotation, scaling, flipping) to improve model robustness.\n   - Use a tree-based structure for hyperparameter optimization.\n   - Consider different model architectures (e.g., MobileNetV3, EfficientNetB0) to balance accuracy and computational cost.\n   - Include wrapper validations for first coarse checks.\n\n5. Computation budget and robustness\n   - Total time budget: 1.5 sf\n   - Major costs: K-Fold training (most expensive part), model validation, hyperparameter tuning, and framework compatibility checks.\n   - Check deadlines periodically within heavy operations and break gracefully.\n   - Fallback solutions: If any validation fails, revert to last stable checkpoint or a pre-trained version.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Evaluate model performance against accuracy, specificity, recall, F1-Score.\n   - Ensuring model meets minimal performance thresholds before return.\n   - Detailed logging of all models tested and hyperparameter settings.",
